Contact-Transition Belief Preservation for Deformable-Object State Estimation: A Theoretical Framework

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

This document formalizes a hybrid Bayesian filtering framework for deformable-object state estimation under contact transitions. The central theoretical object is a measure-valued belief process over a hybrid state space \mathcal{Z} = \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{X}, where \mathcal{M} denotes discrete contact modes and \mathcal{X} denotes continuous physical states comprising geometry, deformation, and material parameters. The framework addresses a specific failure mode—branch loss—where feasible dynamical hypotheses are irreversibly deleted from the belief support during contact transitions. We establish decision-theoretic lower bounds on the excess loss induced by branch loss, prove support-coverage guarantees for a two-stage feasible-carrier construction, derive local convergence conditions for the resulting branching particle filter, and provide finite-sample stratified conformal coverage bounds. Identifiability conditions for material parameters are characterized, and a decision-theoretic abstention rule is derived. The framework is positioned against recent work on support-safe hybrid filtering, conformal prediction in stratified configuration spaces, and visuotactile stiffness estimation.

---

Part I: Mathematical Foundations

1. The Hybrid State Space

Definition 1.1 (Hybrid State). Let the hybrid state space be

\mathcal{Z} = \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{X},

where:

· \mathcal{M} = \{1, \ldots, M\} is a finite set of discrete contact modes (e.g., free, impact, stick, slip, firm-contact).
· \mathcal{X} \subset \mathbb{R}^d is the continuous physical state, decomposed as

x_t = (g_t, d_t, \theta_t) \in \mathcal{G} \times \mathcal{D} \times \Theta,

with g_t geometry (object shape and pose), d_t deformation field (displacement or strain), and \theta_t material parameters (elasticity, friction, plasticity).

The full hybrid state is z_t = (m_t, x_t) \in \mathcal{Z}.

Remark 1.2. This decomposition refines the latent representation of VIRDO++, which decouples the 3D geometric representation of deformable objects into an undeformed (nominal) shape signed-distance field and a set of deformation fields, maintaining a belief distribution over object deformation via a particle filter-based inference algorithm . The variable g_t corresponds to the nominal shape, d_t to the deformation field, and \theta_t to material parameters that VIRDO++ does not explicitly represent as a separate latent variable.

2. Hybrid Dynamics as a Measure-Valued Process

Definition 2.1 (Hybrid Dynamics). The hybrid dynamics are governed by the stochastic differential equation with jumps:

dx_t = f_{m_t}(x_t, a_t)\,dt + \sigma_{m_t}(x_t)\,dW_t + \sum_{k:\, t_k \leq t} J_{m_{t_k^-} \to m_{t_k}}(x_{t_k^-})\,\delta_{t_k},

where:

· f_m: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{A} \to T\mathcal{X} is the mode-conditioned continuous drift.
· \sigma_m is the diffusion coefficient.
· J_{m \to m'}: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{X} is the reset map at contact-mode transitions.
· \delta_{t_k} is the Dirac measure at jump time t_k.

The discrete mode process m_t evolves as a Markov jump process with transition rates \lambda_{m \to m'}(x_t, a_t) depending on the continuous state.

Remark 2.2 (Measure-Valued Nature). Contact-rich robot dynamics are hybrid: a single observation can match several latent states and contact regimes (free, impact, stick–slip) . The jump term \sum J \cdot \delta_{t_k} makes the process measure-valued: at each jump, the state distribution receives an impulse not captured by the continuous flow. This is the mathematical origin of the branch-preservation problem. The measure-valued representation is consistent with the treatment of reset maps and impulse balance in non-smooth dynamical systems .

3. Observability Structure

Definition 3.1 (Observation Process). The observation process is

o_t = h_{m_t}(x_t) + \epsilon_t, \quad \epsilon_t \sim p_\epsilon(\cdot \mid m_t),

with h_m: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{O} the mode-conditioned observation map (vision, tactile, proprioception).

Definition 3.2 (Perceptual Ambiguity Set). The perceptual ambiguity set at time t is

\mathcal{A}_t(o_t) = \left\{ (m, x) \in \mathcal{Z} : \|h_m(x) - o_t\| \leq \rho \right\}

for some tolerance \rho > 0. This set may be multimodal in x and multi-valued in m.

4. The Belief Process

Definition 4.1 (Hybrid Belief). The hybrid belief at time t is a probability measure \pi_t on \mathcal{Z}:

\pi_t(dm, dx) = p(m_t = m \mid o_{0:t}) \cdot p(x_t \in dx \mid m_t = m, o_{0:t}).

Equivalently,

\pi_t = \sum_{m \in \mathcal{M}} w_t(m) \cdot \pi_t^{(m)},

where w_t(m) = p(m_t = m \mid o_{0:t}) and \pi_t^{(m)} is the conditional continuous belief given mode m.

Definition 4.2 (Branch Loss). A belief sequence \{\pi_t\} suffers branch loss at mode m if there exists t' > t such that

w_t(m) > 0 \quad \text{but} \quad w_{t'}(m) = 0

despite mode m remaining physically feasible under the dynamics and observations.

