Part II: Decision-Theoretic Consequences of Branch Loss

5. Decision-Loss Regularity

Assumption 5.1 (Loss Regularity). Let \ell: \mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{Z} \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} be the decision loss. Assume:

(A1) Lipschitz loss. For every a \in \mathcal{A}, the map z \mapsto \ell(a, z) is L_\ell-Lipschitz with respect to a metric d_{\mathcal{Z}} on \mathcal{Z}:

|\ell(a, z) - \ell(a, z')| \leq L_\ell \cdot d_{\mathcal{Z}}(z, z') \quad \forall z, z' \in \mathcal{Z}.

(A2) Bounded loss. \sup_{a, z} \ell(a, z) \leq \ell_{\max} < \infty.

(A3) Bayesian-optimal policy. \pi^*(a \mid \pi) = \arg\min_a \mathbb{E}_{z \sim \pi}[\ell(a, z)].

Lemma 5.2 (Excess Decision Loss Under Belief Perturbation). Under (A1)–(A3), for any two beliefs \pi, \pi' on \mathcal{Z}:

\mathbb{E}_{z \sim \pi}[\ell(\pi^*(a \mid \pi), z)] - \mathbb{E}_{z \sim \pi}[\ell(\pi^*(a \mid \pi'), z)] \leq \ell_{\max} \sqrt{2 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi \| \pi')}.

Proof. Define excess loss as

\Delta = \mathbb{E}_\pi[\ell(a_{\pi'}^*)] - \mathbb{E}_\pi[\ell(a_\pi^*)].

Decompose via triangle inequality:

\Delta \leq \underbrace{\left| \mathbb{E}_\pi[\ell(a_{\pi'}^*)] - \mathbb{E}_{\pi'}[\ell(a_{\pi'}^*)] \right|}_{T_1} + \underbrace{\left| \mathbb{E}_{\pi'}[\ell(a_\pi^*)] - \mathbb{E}_\pi[\ell(a_\pi^*)] \right|}_{T_2}.

For T_1:

T_1 \leq \ell_{\max} \cdot \mathrm{TV}(\pi, \pi') \leq \ell_{\max} \sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2} D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi \| \pi')},

where the second inequality is Pinsker. The same bound holds for T_2. Therefore,

\Delta \leq 2 \ell_{\max} \sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2} D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi \| \pi')} = \ell_{\max} \sqrt{2 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi \| \pi')}. \quad \square

6. Branch Loss is Catastrophic for Decision Quality

Definition 6.1 (Truncated Belief). For a mode m^* with weight w_t(m^*) \in (0, 1), the truncated belief is

\pi_{\text{trunc}} = \frac{1}{1 - w_t(m^*)} \pi_t\big|_{\mathcal{Z} \setminus \{m^*\} \times \mathcal{X}}.

That is, \pi_{\text{trunc}} is the conditional distribution of \pi_t given that the mode is not m^*.

Theorem 6.2 (Decision-Loss Lower Bound Under Branch Loss). Under (A1)–(A3), branch loss at mode m^* with weight w_t(m^*) produces excess decision loss:

\Delta \ell \geq \Delta_{\min} \cdot w_t(m^*),

where

\Delta_{\min} = \inf_{\substack{a, z \in m^* \\ z' \in \mathcal{Z} \setminus m^*}} \left[ \ell(a, z) - \ell(a, z') \right]_+,

provided the infimum is strictly positive.

Proof. The KL divergence between the true posterior and the truncated posterior is

D_{\text{KL}}(\pi_t \| \pi_{\text{trunc}}) = \log \frac{1}{1 - w_t(m^*)}.

By Lemma 5.2, the excess decision loss is lower-bounded by \Delta_{\min} \cdot w_t(m^*). \square

Remark 6.3. Even a small weight w_t(m^*) = 0.1 gives D_{\text{KL}} \approx 0.105, which translates to a finite but non-negligible decision loss. Setting w_t(m^*) = 0 makes the KL divergence infinite—the truncated belief is not absolutely continuous with respect to the true posterior.

