Part III: Identifiability of Physical State Components

7. Observationally Equivalent Sets

Definition 7.1 (Observational Equivalence). Two hybrid states (m, x) and (m', x') are observationally equivalent under observation history o_{0:t} if

p(o_{0:t} \mid m, x) = p(o_{0:t} \mid m', x').

The observationally equivalent set \mathcal{E}(o_{0:t}) is the union of all such states.

Definition 7.2 (Identifiability). A component x^{(i)} of the continuous state is identifiable from observation history o_{0:t} if

(m, x), (m', x') \in \mathcal{E}(o_{0:t}) \implies x^{(i)} = x'^{(i)}.

8. Material Parameter Identifiability

Theorem 8.1 (Contact Excitation Required for Material Identifiability). Let \theta_t \in \Theta be the material parameters. If the action sequence a_{0:t} produces no contact transitions (i.e., m_s = \text{free} for all s \leq t), then \theta_t is not identifiable from o_{0:t}.

Proof. In the free-motion mode, the material parameters \theta enter only through the contact reset maps J_{m \to m'} and the contact-mode dynamics f_{m \neq \text{free}}. If no contact occurs, the observation likelihood p(o_t \mid m = \text{free}, x_t) is independent of \theta. Therefore, the posterior over \theta remains equal to the prior, and no amount of observation in free motion can distinguish different \theta values. \square

Corollary 8.2 (Excitation Requirement). Material parameter identifiability requires contact excitation—a sequence of actions that triggers contact transitions and produces mode-conditioned observations sensitive to \theta.

Remark 8.3. High-resolution visuotactile sensors provide detailed contact information that is promising for inferring the physical properties of objects in contact . However, the identifiability argument establishes that such inference is fundamentally limited without contact excitation, regardless of sensor resolution.

9. Regularized Fisher Rank Test

Lemma 9.1 (Effective Rank Stability). Define the smoothed Fisher matrix

\hat{\mathbf{F}}_\theta^{(\lambda)} = \hat{\mathbf{F}}_\theta + \lambda \mathbf{I}, \quad \lambda > 0.

Compute the effective rank

r_{\text{eff}} = \#\left\{ i : \sigma_i(\hat{\mathbf{F}}_\theta^{(\lambda)}) \geq \tau \cdot \sigma_{\max}(\hat{\mathbf{F}}_\theta^{(\lambda)}) \right\},

where \sigma_i are singular values and \tau \in (0, 1) is a relative tolerance. Let \|\Delta \mathbf{F}\|_2 \leq \eta be the observation-noise-induced perturbation. Then the effective rank difference is integer-valued and bounded by

\left| r_{\text{eff}}(\hat{\mathbf{F}}_\theta) - r_{\text{eff}}(\mathbf{F}_\theta) \right| \leq \#\left\{ i : \sigma_i(\mathbf{F}_\theta) \in [\tau \sigma_{\max} - (\eta + \lambda),\; \tau \sigma_{\max} + (\eta + \lambda)] \right\}.

Proof. By Weyl's inequality, the singular values of \hat{\mathbf{F}}_\theta^{(\lambda)} differ from those of \mathbf{F}_\theta by at most \eta + \lambda. A singular value crossing the threshold \tau \sigma_{\max} changes the effective rank by at most one per crossing; the total change is bounded by the number of singular values in the interval. \square

Interpretation. Instability occurs only when singular values cluster near the threshold. If the spectrum is well-separated, the rank is stable. If values cluster near \tau \sigma_{\max}, the identifiability verdict is unreliable.

10. Geometry/Deformation Identifiability Under Occlusion

Theorem 10.1 (Occlusion-Induced Ambiguity). Let \mathcal{G}_{\text{occluded}} \subset \mathcal{G} be the set of geometries consistent with the unoccluded observation. If the occlusion mask is non-trivial (i.e., has positive measure), then |\mathcal{E}(o_t)| > 1 generically.

Proof. The observation map h_m: \mathcal{G} \times \mathcal{D} \to \mathcal{O} is generally non-injective when restricted to occluded regions. For any occlusion mask K \subset \mathcal{O} with \mu(K) > 0, there exist distinct (g, d) \neq (g', d') such that h_m(g, d) = h_m(g', d') on the visible region \mathcal{O} \setminus K. \square

