Part IV: Support-Safe Filtering and Branch Preservation

11. The Support-Safe Proposal

Definition 11.1 (Feasible Transition Carrier). Define the feasible transition carrier

\mathcal{F}_t(z_{t-1}) = \left\{ z_t \in \mathcal{Z} : \exists a_{t-1} \text{ s.t. } z_t \in \text{Supp}(p(z_t \mid z_{t-1}, a_{t-1})) \right\}.

Definition 11.2 (Support-Safe Proposal). Let q_\psi(z_t \mid h_t) be a learned amortized proposal. The support-safe proposal is

q_{\text{safe}}(z_t \mid z_{t-1}) = (1 - \epsilon) \cdot q_\psi(z_t \mid h_t) + \epsilon \cdot \frac{\mathbb{1}[z_t \in \mathcal{F}_t(z_{t-1})]}{|\mathcal{F}_t(z_{t-1})|}.

This mechanism, introduced by VHyDRO, mixes the learned proposal with a feasible transition law before sampling and importance weighting, ensuring that every transition retained by the model-feasible carrier remains covered .

12. Finite Support Approximation

Construction 12.1 (Projected Feasible Carrier). When \mathcal{X} is infinite-dimensional, define the projected feasible carrier

\mathcal{F}_t^{(J)} = \left\{ \Phi_j(z_t) : z_t \in \mathcal{F}_t,\; j \in \{1, \ldots, J\} \right\},

where \{\Phi_j\} are finite-dimensional projections corresponding to retained modes and sensor channels.

Assumption 12.2. Assume:

(B1) The projection family \{\Phi_j\} is Lipschitz-invertible with constant L_\Phi:

d_{\mathcal{Z}}(z, \Phi^{-1}(\Phi(z))) \leq L_\Phi \cdot \mathrm{diam}(\Phi(\mathcal{Z})).

(B2) The true feasible carrier is Hausdorff-close to its projection:

d_H(\mathcal{F}_t, \Phi^{-1}(\mathcal{F}_t^{(J)})) \leq \delta_J.

Lemma 12.3 (Approximation Error). Under (B1)–(B2),

\mathrm{TV}\!\left( \mu_{\mathcal{F}_t}^{(J)}, \mathrm{Unif}(\mathcal{F}_t) \right) \leq L_\Phi \cdot \delta_J.

Proof. The total variation between a measure and its push-forward under a Lipschitz-invertible map is bounded by the Hausdorff distance between the underlying sets multiplied by the Lipschitz constant. \square

13. Support Coverage Guarantee

Theorem 13.1 (Support Coverage). Under the support-safe proposal with finite carrier \mathcal{F}_t^{(J)},

q_{\text{safe}}(z_t \mid z_{t-1}) \geq \frac{\epsilon}{|\mathcal{F}_t^{(J)}|} \quad \text{for all } z_t \in \Phi^{-1}(\mathcal{F}_t^{(J)}).

The residual mass on \mathcal{F}_t \setminus \Phi^{-1}(\mathcal{F}_t^{(J)}) is bounded by \epsilon \cdot L_\Phi \delta_J.

Proof. Direct from Definition 11.2: the second term is positive for any feasible z_t, and the mixture assigns at least \epsilon \cdot |\mathcal{F}_t^{(J)}|^{-1} probability. \square

Corollary 13.2 (No Branch Loss). Under support-safe filtering, w_t(m) > 0 for all modes m that remain feasible under the dynamics.

Proof. By induction. If w_{t-1}(m) > 0 for feasible m, then the support-safe proposal assigns positive probability to at least one state in mode m at time t. The importance weight update preserves this positivity. \square

14. Two-Stage Feasible Carrier

Construction 14.1 (Two-Stage Decomposition). Decompose

\mathcal{F}_t = \mathcal{F}_t^{\text{mode}} \times \mathcal{F}_t^{\text{def}},

where

\mathcal{F}_t^{\text{mode}} = \{m' : \lambda_{m \to m'}(x_{t-1}) > 0\}

is the finite, enumerable set of feasible mode transitions (at most M for the contact-mode set), and

\mathcal{F}_t^{\text{def}} = \left\{ x_t = x_{t-1} + f_m \Delta t + \sigma_m \sqrt{\Delta t}\,\xi + J_{m \to m'}(x_{t-1}) : \xi \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I) \right\}

is the deformation reachable set approximated by K dynamics rollouts.

Algorithm 14.2 (Two-Stage Support-Safe Procedure).

1. Mode coverage: Always keep \geq 1 particle per feasible m' \in \mathcal{F}_t^{\text{mode}}.
2. Deformation coverage: For modes with depleted particles, sample from the reset map J_{m \to m'} + noise, not from the learned q_\psi.

This makes branch preservation implementable: the mode set is finite and enumerable; the deformation set is approximated by a fixed number of rollouts.

15. Convergence Under Local Minorization

Assumption 15.1 (Local Minorization). There exists a compact set \mathcal{K} \subset \mathcal{Z}, \epsilon_{\min} > 0, and a probability measure \nu on \mathcal{K} such that for every z_{t-1} \in \mathcal{K}:

p(z_t \in A \mid z_{t-1}, a_{t-1}) \geq \epsilon_{\min} \, \nu(A) \quad \forall A \subseteq \mathcal{K}.

Assumption 15.2 (Drift Condition). There exists a Lyapunov function V: \mathcal{Z} \to [1, \infty) and constants \alpha \in (0, 1), \beta < \infty such that

\mathbb{E}[V(z_t) \mid z_{t-1}] \leq \alpha V(z_{t-1}) + \beta.

Theorem 15.3 (Convergence Rate). Under Assumptions 15.1–15.2 and the amortization gap bound D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_t \| q_\psi) \leq \epsilon_{\text{amort}}, the branching particle filter satisfies

\left\| \mathbb{E}_{\pi_t}[f] - \mathbb{E}_{\hat{\pi}_t}[f] \right\|_\infty \leq C_{\mathcal{K}} \sqrt{\frac{\log N}{N}} + 2 \sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{amort}}} + \epsilon_{\text{out}},

where C_{\mathcal{K}} = 2 \ell_{\max} / (\epsilon_{\min} \ell_{\min}) on \mathcal{K}, and \epsilon_{\text{out}} is the probability of being outside \mathcal{K}, controlled by the drift condition.

Remark 15.4. Assumption 15.1 is a local condition that holds on compact subsets where the diffusion coefficient is bounded below and the reset maps have bounded Jacobians. It will not hold globally—if the robot is in free space, it cannot jump to deep penetration. The constant C_{\mathcal{K}} will be large in practice, and the convergence rate is illustrative, not a practical guarantee.

