Part V: Conformal Calibration for Contact-Stratified Coverage

16. Stratified Conformal Calibration

Definition 16.1 (Stratified Calibration). Partition the calibration data by contact mode stratum s \in \mathcal{S} = \{\text{free}, \text{impact}, \text{stick}, \text{slip}, \text{firm}\}. For each stratum s, compute the conformal quantile

\hat{q}_s = \text{Quantile}_{1-\alpha}\left( \left\{ V_i : z_i \in \text{stratum } s \right\} \right),

where V_i = \|z_i - \hat{z}_i\| is the nonconformity score.

Theorem 16.2 (Stratified Marginal Coverage). Under the assumption that calibration and test units within each stratum are exchangeable, the stratified conformal prediction set satisfies

P(z_t^* \in \mathcal{C}_{1-\alpha}^{(s)} \mid z_t^* \in \text{stratum } s) \geq 1 - \alpha.

Proof. Within each stratum, the conformal calibration procedure is standard: the nonconformity scores are exchangeable, and the rank of the test score is uniform. Therefore, the coverage probability within each stratum is at least 1-\alpha. \square

Lemma 16.3 (Finite-Sample Coverage Gap). Under exchangeability of calibration and test points within stratum s, the finite-sample coverage satisfies

P\!\left( z^* \in \mathcal{C}^{(s)}_{1-\alpha_s} \right) \geq 1 - \alpha_s - \sqrt{\frac{\log(2/\delta_s)}{2 n_s}}

with probability at least 1 - \delta_s, for any \delta_s \in (0, 1).

Proof. By the Dvoretzky–Kiefer–Wolfowitz inequality applied to the empirical CDF of the scores:

P\!\left( \sup_{q} \left| \hat{F}_s(q) - F_s(q) \right| > \epsilon_s \right) \leq 2 e^{-2 n_s \epsilon_s^2}.

Setting \epsilon_s = \sqrt{\log(2/\delta_s) / (2 n_s)} and translating the CDF deviation into a coverage gap yields the result. \square

Corollary 16.4 (Total Gap Across Strata). With a union bound over |\mathcal{S}| strata:

P\!\left( \forall s \in \mathcal{S} : z^* \in \mathcal{C}^{(s)}_{1-\alpha_s} \right) \geq 1 - \sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}} \left( \alpha_s + \sqrt{\frac{\log(2|\mathcal{S}|/\delta)}{2 n_s}} \right).

Practical Requirement. To achieve a finite-sample gap of \epsilon_s = 0.02 at \delta = 0.05, one needs

n_s \geq \frac{\log(2 |\mathcal{S}| / 0.05)}{2 \cdot 0.02^2} \approx 1{,}800 \text{ calibration points per stratum}.

Remark 16.5. With |\mathcal{S}| = 5, this implies \geq 9{,}000 calibrated contact transitions. Current datasets such as SoftVTBench contain 4,000 expert demonstrations across 40 tasks with synchronized visuo-tactile observations and evaluator-only contact states , but they are not labeled by contact stratum at transition level. The 9,000-transition requirement exceeds what is currently available without manual annotation.

17. Conditional Coverage Limitation

Theorem 17.1 (Conditional Coverage is Not Guaranteed by Stratification). Stratified conformal prediction does not provide pointwise conditional coverage:

P(z_t^* \in \mathcal{C}_{1-\alpha} \mid z_t^* = z) \geq 1 - \alpha \quad \text{for all } z.

Proof. Conditional coverage requires the prediction set to be valid for every possible state z, which is impossible with a finite calibration set unless the nonconformity score is monotonically related to the conditional density. In general, the best achievable is group-conditional coverage (coverage within strata), not pointwise-conditional coverage. \square

Remark 17.2. CaPTURe generates probabilistically valid prediction regions of the unknown future system configuration using particle-based models of arbitrary fidelity, handling trans-dimensional uncertainty by incorporating stratified configuration spaces (e.g., free space, contact manifolds) . The stratified conformal framework extends this approach to the contact-mode strata specific to deformable-object manipulation.

