Part VI: Decision-Theoretic Value of Belief Preservation

18. Value of Information in the Hybrid POMDP

Definition 18.1 (Hybrid POMDP). The hybrid POMDP is defined by (\mathcal{Z}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{O}, T, R, \gamma). The belief-space value function is

V^*(\pi) = \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \left[ R(\pi, a) + \gamma \int V^*(\tau(\pi, a, o))\,p(o \mid \pi, a)\,do \right],

where \tau(\pi, a, o) is the Bayesian belief update.

Definition 18.2 (Value of Belief Preservation). For a belief \pi and an action a, the value of belief preservation is

\text{VoBP}(\pi, a) = V^*(\pi) - V^*(\pi_{\text{collapsed}}),

where \pi_{\text{collapsed}} is a collapsed (e.g., MAP) belief.

19. Branch Preservation Improves Decision Quality

Theorem 19.1 (Value of Branch Preservation). Let \pi be a multimodal belief with two modes m_1, m_2 that are observationally indistinguishable under the current action, and let \pi_{\text{collapsed}} = \delta_{m_1}. Then

\text{VoBP}(\pi, a) \geq \gamma \cdot \Delta_{\min} \cdot w(m_2).

Proof. Under the multimodal belief, the policy can select an informative action that actively disambiguates between modes. Under the collapsed belief, the policy cannot select an informative action because it does not know that ambiguity exists. The value difference is the expected benefit of information acquisition:

\text{VoBP} = \mathbb{E}[\text{value of information}] \geq \gamma \cdot \Delta_{\min} \cdot w(m_2),

where the inequality follows from the fact that the value of perfect information is at least the decision loss avoided by knowing the true mode. \square

20. Decision-Theoretic Abstention

Definition 20.1 (Abstention Value). Define the abstention value

V_{\text{abstain}}(\pi_t) = \max_{a_{\text{info}} \in \mathcal{A}_{\text{info}}} \; \mathbb{E}_{o \sim p(\cdot \mid \pi_t, a_{\text{info}})} \left[ V^*(\tau(\pi_t, a_{\text{info}}, o)) \right] - V^*(\pi_t),

where \mathcal{A}_{\text{info}} \subseteq \mathcal{A} is the set of informative actions that maximize expected information gain about the mode posterior.

Lemma 20.2 (Optimal Abstention Rule). Under the same regularity as Lemma 5.2, abstention is optimal if and only if

|\mathcal{C}_{1-\alpha}| > B_{\max}^* \quad \text{with} \quad B_{\max}^* = \frac{V_{\text{abstain}}(\pi_t)}{\kappa \cdot \alpha},

where \kappa > 0 is the marginal decision cost per unit of prediction-set volume.

Proof. The decision value of abstaining equals V_{\text{abstain}}. The decision cost of acting on a set of size |\mathcal{C}| is bounded below by \kappa \cdot \alpha \cdot |\mathcal{C}| (larger sets imply lower confidence and higher miscoverage risk). Abstention is optimal precisely when the value exceeds the cost. \square

