30. Conclusion

This document establishes the theoretical foundations of contact-transition belief preservation for deformable-object state estimation. The core mathematical contribution is the formalization of branch preservation as a decision-theoretic necessity, with provable guarantees under a two-stage support-safe proposal. The architecture maintains a measure-valued posterior over discrete contact modes and continuous deformation states, with stratified conformal calibration and decision-theoretic action selection.

The boundary between theorem and assumption is explicit. What remains to be proven—OOD coverage transfer, sub-millisecond latency, non-stationary material tracking—is acknowledged as open. The first experiment is designed to falsify the primary hypothesis, not to confirm it. A rigorous negative result is scientifically valuable; a positive result that collapses under scrutiny is not.

