Appendix A: Measure-Theoretic Foundations

A.1 Notation and Conventions

Let (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P}) be a complete probability space. For a Polish space \mathcal{S}, let \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{S}) denote its Borel \sigma-algebra, \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}) the space of Borel probability measures on \mathcal{S}, and C_b(\mathcal{S}) the space of bounded continuous real-valued functions on \mathcal{S}, equipped with the supremum norm \|\cdot\|_\infty.

For a measure \mu \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}) and a measurable map T: \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{S}', the pushforward is T_\# \mu(A) = \mu(T^{-1}(A)).

Total variation distance:

\mathrm{TV}(\mu, \nu) = \sup_{A \in \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{S})} |\mu(A) - \nu(A)| = \frac{1}{2} \int |d\mu - d\nu|.

Kullback–Leibler divergence: For \mu \ll \nu,

D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\mu \| \nu) = \int \log \frac{d\mu}{d\nu} \, d\mu,

and D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\mu \| \nu) = +\infty otherwise.

Wasserstein-2 distance: For \mu, \nu \in \mathcal{P}_2(\mathcal{S}) (measures with finite second moment),

\mathcal{W}_2^2(\mu, \nu) = \inf_{\gamma \in \Gamma(\mu, \nu)} \int_{\mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{S}} d_{\mathcal{S}}(x, y)^2 \, d\gamma(x, y),

where \Gamma(\mu, \nu) is the set of couplings.

Pinsker's inequality. For \mu, \nu \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}),

\mathrm{TV}(\mu, \nu) \leq \sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2} D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\mu \| \nu)}.

Kantorovich–Rubinstein duality. For \mu, \nu \in \mathcal{P}_1(\mathcal{S}),

\mathcal{W}_1(\mu, \nu) = \sup_{\|f\|_{\mathrm{Lip}} \leq 1} \left| \int f \, d\mu - \int f \, d\nu \right|.

Inequality \mathrm{TV} \leq \mathcal{W}_1. For probability measures on a Polish space,

\mathrm{TV}(\mu, \nu) \leq \mathcal{W}_1(\mu, \nu).

A.2 Measurability of the Hybrid Belief

Proposition A.2.1. Let \mathcal{Z} = \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{X} be the hybrid state space with \mathcal{M} finite and \mathcal{X} a Polish space. Then \mathcal{Z} is Polish under the product topology, and the hybrid belief \pi_t \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Z}) is well-defined.

Proof. A finite product of Polish spaces is Polish. The belief update map \tau: \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Z}) \times \mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{O} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Z}) defined by

\tau(\pi, a, o)(A) = \frac{\int_A p(o \mid z) \, p(dz \mid \pi, a)}{\int_{\mathcal{Z}} p(o \mid z) \, p(dz \mid \pi, a)}

is measurable in (a, o) for \pi-a.e. z by the measurability of the observation kernel p(o \mid z) in (o, z) and the fact that the denominator is positive on the support of the observation likelihood. \square

A.3 Disintegration of the Hybrid Belief

Proposition A.3.1 (Disintegration). Every hybrid belief \pi_t \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Z}) admits a unique disintegration

\pi_t(dm, dx) = w_t(m) \, \pi_t^{(m)}(dx),

where w_t(m) = \pi_t(\{m\} \times \mathcal{X}) and \pi_t^{(m)} \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X}) for each m with w_t(m) > 0.

Proof. Since \mathcal{M} is finite, the marginal w_t(m) is well-defined for each m \in \mathcal{M}. For each m with w_t(m) > 0, define \pi_t^{(m)}(A) = \pi_t(\{m\} \times A) / w_t(m). The result follows from the Radon–Nikodym theorem applied to the conditional measure: for any bounded measurable f: \mathcal{Z} \to \mathbb{R},

\int_{\mathcal{Z}} f \, d\pi_t = \sum_{m \in \mathcal{M}} \int_{\mathcal{X}} f(m, x) \, w_t(m) \, \pi_t^{(m)}(dx).

Uniqueness follows because the factorization is determined by the conditional expectation \mathbb{E}[\mathbb{1}_{A} \mid m] for A \in \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{X}). \square

A.4 Support of the Belief

Definition A.4.1 (Belief Support). The support of \pi_t is

\mathrm{Supp}(\pi_t) = \left\{ z \in \mathcal{Z} : \pi_t(B_\epsilon(z)) > 0 \text{ for every } \epsilon > 0 \right\},

where B_\epsilon(z) is the open ball of radius \epsilon around z in the metric d_{\mathcal{Z}}.

Proposition A.4.2 (Support Conservation). Under the support-safe proposal with \epsilon > 0, if z_{t-1} \in \mathrm{Supp}(\pi_{t-1}), then \mathrm{Supp}(\pi_t) \supseteq \mathcal{F}_t(z_{t-1}) \cap \Phi^{-1}(\mathcal{F}_t^{(J)}).

Proof. By Theorem 13.1, the support-safe proposal assigns mass at least \epsilon / |\mathcal{F}_t^{(J)}| to every state in \Phi^{-1}(\mathcal{F}_t^{(J)}). The importance weight update multiplies this by the observation likelihood p(h_t \mid z_t), which is strictly positive on the support of the observation kernel. Therefore, the posterior assigns positive mass to every z_t \in \mathcal{F}_t(z_{t-1}) \cap \Phi^{-1}(\mathcal{F}_t^{(J)}). \square

