Appendix B: Full Proofs

B.1 Proof of Lemma 5.2 (Excess Decision Loss Under Belief Perturbation)

Lemma 5.2. Under (A1)–(A3), for any two beliefs \pi, \pi' on \mathcal{Z}:

\mathbb{E}_{z \sim \pi}[\ell(\pi^*(a \mid \pi), z)] - \mathbb{E}_{z \sim \pi}[\ell(\pi^*(a \mid \pi'), z)] \leq \ell_{\max} \sqrt{2 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi \| \pi')}.

Proof. Let a_\pi^* = \pi^*(a \mid \pi) and a_{\pi'}^* = \pi^*(a \mid \pi'). Define

\Delta = \mathbb{E}_\pi[\ell(a_{\pi'}^*, z)] - \mathbb{E}_\pi[\ell(a_\pi^*, z)].

Since a_\pi^* minimizes \mathbb{E}_\pi[\ell(a, z)], we have \Delta \geq 0. Decompose

\Delta = \underbrace{\left( \mathbb{E}_\pi[\ell(a_{\pi'}^*, z)] - \mathbb{E}_{\pi'}[\ell(a_{\pi'}^*, z)] \right)}_{T_1} + \underbrace{\left( \mathbb{E}_{\pi'}[\ell(a_{\pi'}^*, z)] - \mathbb{E}_\pi[\ell(a_\pi^*, z)] \right)}_{T_2}.

For T_1, by (A2) and the definition of total variation,

|T_1| \leq \ell_{\max} \cdot \mathrm{TV}(\pi, \pi').

For T_2, note that \mathbb{E}_{\pi'}[\ell(a_{\pi'}^*, z)] \leq \mathbb{E}_{\pi'}[\ell(a_\pi^*, z)] by optimality of a_{\pi'}^* under \pi'. Therefore,

T_2 \leq \mathbb{E}_{\pi'}[\ell(a_\pi^*, z)] - \mathbb{E}_\pi[\ell(a_\pi^*, z)] \leq \ell_{\max} \cdot \mathrm{TV}(\pi, \pi').

Combining:

\Delta \leq 2 \ell_{\max} \cdot \mathrm{TV}(\pi, \pi').

By Pinsker's inequality, \mathrm{TV}(\pi, \pi') \leq \sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2} D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi \| \pi')}. Therefore,

\Delta \leq \ell_{\max} \sqrt{2 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi \| \pi')}. \quad \square

B.2 Proof of Theorem 6.2 (Decision-Loss Lower Bound Under Branch Loss)

Theorem 6.2. Under (A1)–(A3), branch loss at mode m^* with weight w_t(m^*) produces excess decision loss

\Delta \ell \geq \Delta_{\min} \cdot w_t(m^*),

where

\Delta_{\min} = \inf_{\substack{a, z \in m^* \\ z' \in \mathcal{Z} \setminus m^*}} \left[ \ell(a, z) - \ell(a, z') \right]_+.

Proof. Let \pi_t be the true belief and \pi_{\text{trunc}} the truncated belief defined in Definition 6.1. The Radon–Nikodym derivative of \pi_t with respect to \pi_{\text{trunc}} does not exist on \{m^*\} \times \mathcal{X} because \pi_{\text{trunc}}(\{m^*\} \times \mathcal{X}) = 0 while \pi_t(\{m^*\} \times \mathcal{X}) = w_t(m^*) > 0. Therefore, D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_t \| \pi_{\text{trunc}}) = +\infty.

Since the decision loss is bounded by \ell_{\max}, the excess loss is finite even though the KL divergence is infinite. To obtain a lower bound, consider the optimal action under \pi_t:

a_{\pi_t}^* = \arg\min_a \left[ (1 - w_t(m^*)) \mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\text{trunc}}}[\ell(a, z)] + w_t(m^*) \mathbb{E}_{\pi_t^{(m^*)}}[\ell(a, z)] \right].

Under \pi_{\text{trunc}}, the optimal action is

a_{\pi_{\text{trunc}}}^* = \arg\min_a \mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\text{trunc}}}[\ell(a, z)].

By definition of \Delta_{\min}, for any action a,

\mathbb{E}_{\pi_t^{(m^*)}}[\ell(a, z)] - \mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\text{trunc}}}[\ell(a, z)] \geq \Delta_{\min}.

The excess loss is

\Delta \ell = \mathbb{E}_{\pi_t}[\ell(a_{\pi_{\text{trunc}}}^*, z)] - \mathbb{E}_{\pi_t}[\ell(a_{\pi_t}^*, z)] \geq w_t(m^*) \cdot \Delta_{\min}. \quad \square

B.3 Proof of Lemma 12.3 (Approximation Error)

Lemma 12.3. Under (B1)–(B2),

\mathrm{TV}\!\left( \mu_{\mathcal{F}_t}^{(J)}, \mathrm{Unif}(\mathcal{F}_t) \right) \leq L_\Phi \cdot \delta_J.

Proof. Let \mu = \mathrm{Unif}(\mathcal{F}_t) and \mu^{(J)} = \mu_{\mathcal{F}_t}^{(J)}. By the Kantorovich–Rubinstein duality,

\mathcal{W}_1(\mu, \mu^{(J)}) = \sup_{\|f\|_{\mathrm{Lip}} \leq 1} \left| \int f \, d\mu - \int f \, d\mu^{(J)} \right|.

Since \Phi is Lipschitz-invertible with constant L_\Phi and d_H(\mathcal{F}_t, \Phi^{-1}(\mathcal{F}_t^{(J)})) \leq \delta_J, the optimal coupling between \mu and \mu^{(J)} has cost at most L_\Phi \delta_J. Therefore, \mathcal{W}_1(\mu, \mu^{(J)}) \leq L_\Phi \delta_J.

By the inequality \mathrm{TV} \leq \mathcal{W}_1 (valid for probability measures on Polish spaces), we obtain

\mathrm{TV}(\mu, \mu^{(J)}) \leq L_\Phi \delta_J. \quad \square

B.4 Proof of Theorem 13.1 (Support Coverage)

Theorem 13.1. Under the support-safe proposal with finite carrier \mathcal{F}_t^{(J)},

q_{\text{safe}}(z_t \mid z_{t-1}) \geq \frac{\epsilon}{|\mathcal{F}_t^{(J)}|} \quad \text{for all } z_t \in \Phi^{-1}(\mathcal{F}_t^{(J)}).

Proof. By Definition 11.2,

q_{\text{safe}}(z_t \mid z_{t-1}) = (1 - \epsilon) q_\psi(z_t \mid h_t) + \epsilon \frac{\mathbb{1}[z_t \in \mathcal{F}_t(z_{t-1})]}{|\mathcal{F}_t(z_{t-1})|}.

For z_t \in \Phi^{-1}(\mathcal{F}_t^{(J)}), the indicator is 1. Since q_\psi \geq 0,

q_{\text{safe}}(z_t \mid z_{t-1}) \geq \epsilon \cdot \frac{1}{|\mathcal{F}_t(z_{t-1})|} \geq \frac{\epsilon}{|\mathcal{F}_t^{(J)}|},

where the last inequality follows from |\mathcal{F}_t^{(J)}| \geq |\mathcal{F}_t(z_{t-1})| under the projection. \square

Corollary 13.2 (No Branch Loss). Under support-safe filtering, w_t(m) > 0 for all modes m that remain feasible under the dynamics.

Proof. By induction. If w_{t-1}(m) > 0 for feasible m, then the support-safe proposal assigns positive probability to at least one state in mode m at time t. The importance weight update preserves this positivity because the observation likelihood is strictly positive on the support of the observation kernel. \square

B.5 Proof of Theorem 15.3 (Convergence Rate)

Theorem 15.3. Under Assumptions 15.1–15.2 and D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_t \| q_\psi) \leq \epsilon_{\text{amort}},

\left\| \mathbb{E}_{\pi_t}[f] - \mathbb{E}_{\hat{\pi}_t}[f] \right\|_\infty \leq C_{\mathcal{K}} \sqrt{\frac{\log N}{N}} + 2 \sqrt{\epsilon_{\text{amort}}} + \epsilon_{\text{out}},

where C_{\mathcal{K}} = 2 \ell_{\max} / (\epsilon_{\min} \ell_{\min}).

Proof. The proof proceeds in four steps.

Step 1 (Local minorization implies geometric ergodicity on \mathcal{K}). Under Assumption 15.1, the transition kernel P restricted to \mathcal{K} satisfies

P(z, A) \geq \epsilon_{\min} \nu(A) \quad \forall z \in \mathcal{K}, A \subseteq \mathcal{K}.

This is the standard Doeblin minorization condition. By the Doeblin theorem, P has a unique invariant measure \nu_{\mathcal{K}} on \mathcal{K}, and

\|P^n(z, \cdot) - \nu_{\mathcal{K}}\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \leq (1 - \epsilon_{\min})^n.

Step 2 (Particle filter convergence on \mathcal{K}). By the standard convergence theory for branching particle filters, under minorization on \mathcal{K} with observation likelihood bounded in [\ell_{\min}, \ell_{\max}],

\left\| \mathbb{E}_{\pi_t}[f] - \mathbb{E}_{\hat{\pi}_t}[f] \right\|_\infty \leq \frac{\|f\|_\infty \ell_{\max}}{\epsilon_{\min} \ell_{\min}} \sqrt{\frac{\log N}{N}}

for f \in C_b(\mathcal{K}).

Step 3 (Extension outside \mathcal{K} via drift). Under Assumption 15.2, the Lyapunov function V controls the escape probability:

\mathbb{P}(z_t \notin \mathcal{K}) \leq \frac{\alpha^t V(z_0) + \beta / (1 - \alpha)}{\inf_{z \notin \mathcal{K}} V(z)} =: \epsilon_{\text{out}}.

By the triangle inequality,

\left\| \mathbb{E}_{\pi_t}[f] - \mathbb{E}_{\hat{\pi}_t}[f] \right\|_\infty \leq \left\| \mathbb{E}_{\pi_t^{\mathcal{K}}}[f] - \mathbb{E}_{\hat{\pi}_t^{\mathcal{K}}}[f] \right\|_\infty + 2 \|f\|_\infty \epsilon_{\text{out}}.

Step 4 (Amortization gap). The KL divergence between the true posterior and the amortized proposal bounds the bias:

\left| \mathbb{E}_{\pi_t}[f] - \mathbb{E}_{q_\psi}[f] \right| \leq \|f\|_\infty \sqrt{2 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_t \| q_\psi)} \leq \|f\|_\infty \sqrt{2 \epsilon_{\text{amort}}}.

Combining Steps 2–4 and absorbing \|f\|_\infty into the constant yields the stated bound. \square

Remark B.5.1. Assumption 15.1 is a local condition. It holds on compact subsets where the diffusion coefficient is bounded below and the reset maps have bounded Jacobians. It will not hold globally on \mathcal{Z}: if the robot is in free space, the transition kernel cannot assign positive probability to deep penetration in one step. The constant C_{\mathcal{K}} depends on \mathcal{K} and may be large; the rate is conditional, not universal.

B.6 Proof of Theorem 16.2 (Stratified Marginal Coverage)

Theorem 16.2. Under exchangeability within each stratum,

P(z_t^* \in \mathcal{C}_{1-\alpha}^{(s)} \mid z_t^* \in \text{stratum } s) \geq 1 - \alpha.

Proof. Fix stratum s with calibration scores V_1, \ldots, V_{n_s} and test score V^*. Under exchangeability of the n_s + 1 scores,

\text{rank}(V^*) \sim \mathrm{Unif}\{1, \ldots, n_s + 1\}.

The conformal quantile is \hat{q}_s = V_{(\lceil (1-\alpha)(n_s + 1) \rceil)}, the \lceil (1-\alpha)(n_s + 1) \rceil-th smallest calibration score. Then

P(V^* \leq \hat{q}_s) = P(\text{rank}(V^*) \leq \lceil (1-\alpha)(n_s + 1) \rceil) = \frac{\lceil (1-\alpha)(n_s + 1) \rceil}{n_s + 1} \geq 1 - \alpha. \quad \square

B.7 Proof of Lemma 16.3 (Finite-Sample Coverage Gap)

Lemma 16.3. Under exchangeability of calibration and test points within stratum s,

P\!\left( z^* \in \mathcal{C}^{(s)}_{1-\alpha_s} \right) \geq 1 - \alpha_s - \sqrt{\frac{\log(2/\delta_s)}{2 n_s}}

with probability at least 1 - \delta_s.

Proof. Let F_s be the true CDF of the nonconformity scores and \hat{F}_s the empirical CDF. By the Dvoretzky–Kiefer–Wolfowitz inequality,

P\!\left( \sup_q |\hat{F}_s(q) - F_s(q)| > \epsilon_s \right) \leq 2 e^{-2 n_s \epsilon_s^2}.

Setting \epsilon_s = \sqrt{\log(2/\delta_s) / (2 n_s)} gives P(\sup_q |\hat{F}_s - F_s| > \epsilon_s) \leq \delta_s.

On the event \{\sup_q |\hat{F}_s - F_s| \leq \epsilon_s\}, the empirical quantile \hat{q}_s satisfies F_s(\hat{q}_s) \geq 1 - \alpha_s - \epsilon_s. Therefore,

P(z^* \in \mathcal{C}^{(s)}_{1-\alpha_s}) = F_s(\hat{q}_s) \geq 1 - \alpha_s - \epsilon_s. \quad \square

B.8 Proof of Theorem 17.1 (Conditional Coverage Impossibility)

Theorem 17.1. Stratified conformal prediction does not provide pointwise conditional coverage:

P(z_t^* \in \mathcal{C}_{1-\alpha} \mid z_t^* = z) \geq 1 - \alpha \quad \text{for all } z.

Proof. Suppose, for contradiction, that pointwise conditional coverage held. Then for every z, the prediction set \mathcal{C}_{1-\alpha} must contain z with probability at least 1-\alpha. Consider a partition of \mathcal{Z} into countably many disjoint events E_1, E_2, \ldots with P(E_k) > 0. For each E_k, conditional coverage would require

P(z_t^* \in \mathcal{C}_{1-\alpha} \mid z_t^* \in E_k) \geq 1 - \alpha.

This is stronger than marginal coverage and cannot be verified from a finite calibration set unless the nonconformity score is monotonically related to the conditional density of the test point. In general, with a finite calibration set of size n, the best achievable is a uniform bound over a partition of size O(\sqrt{n}) (via VC-style arguments), not pointwise. \square

B.9 Proof of Theorem 19.1 (Value of Branch Preservation)

Theorem 19.1. Let \pi be a multimodal belief with two modes m_1, m_2 that are observationally indistinguishable under the current action, and let \pi_{\text{collapsed}} = \delta_{m_1}. Then

\text{VoBP}(\pi, a) \geq \gamma \cdot \Delta_{\min} \cdot w(m_2).

Proof. Let V^*(\pi) be the optimal value under belief \pi. Since \pi is multimodal, the optimal policy can select an informative action a_{\text{info}} that maximizes the expected information gain about the mode. Under \pi_{\text{collapsed}}, the policy is forced to act as if mode m_1 is certain, and cannot select an informative action (because it does not know that ambiguity exists).

The value difference is

V^*(\pi) - V^*(\pi_{\text{collapsed}}) \geq \gamma \left( V^*(\tau(\pi, a_{\text{info}}, o)) - V^*(\pi_{\text{collapsed}}) \right).

After the informative action, the observation o disambiguates the mode. The expected value of disambiguation is at least \Delta_{\min} w(m_2), the decision loss avoided by knowing the true mode. Therefore,

\text{VoBP}(\pi, a) \geq \gamma \cdot \Delta_{\min} \cdot w(m_2). \quad \square

B.10 Proof of Lemma 20.2 (Optimal Abstention Rule)

Lemma 20.2. Under the same regularity as Lemma 5.2, abstention is optimal if and only if

|\mathcal{C}_{1-\alpha}| > B_{\max}^* = \frac{V_{\text{abstain}}(\pi_t)}{\kappa \cdot \alpha}.

Proof. Let a_{\text{act}} be the best action under the current belief, and a_{\text{abstain}} the abstention action. The value of acting is

V_{\text{act}} = \mathbb{E}_{\pi_t}[\ell(a_{\text{act}}, z)] + \gamma \mathbb{E}_o[V^*(\tau(\pi_t, a_{\text{act}}, o))].

The value of abstaining is

V_{\text{abstain}} = \max_{a_{\text{info}}} \mathbb{E}_o[V^*(\tau(\pi_t, a_{\text{info}}, o))] - V^*(\pi_t).

The cost of acting under a prediction set of size |\mathcal{C}| is bounded below by \kappa \alpha |\mathcal{C}|, where \kappa is the marginal decision cost per unit of set volume and \alpha is the miscoverage level. Abstention is optimal precisely when

V_{\text{abstain}} > \kappa \alpha |\mathcal{C}|,

which rearranges to the stated condition. \square

B.11 Proof of Theorem 8.1 (Contact Excitation Required for Material Identifiability)

Theorem 8.1. Let \theta_t \in \Theta be the material parameters. If the action sequence a_{0:t} produces no contact transitions (i.e., m_s = \text{free} for all s \leq t), then \theta_t is not identifiable from o_{0:t}.

Proof. In free-motion mode, the observation likelihood p(o_s \mid m_s = \text{free}, x_s) depends on the free-mode dynamics f_{\text{free}} and diffusion \sigma_{\text{free}}, which do not depend on the material parameters \theta. The material parameters enter only through the contact reset maps J_{m \to m'} and the contact-mode dynamics f_{m \neq \text{free}}. If no contact occurs, the observation likelihood is conditionally independent of \theta given the free-mode state. Therefore, the posterior over \theta remains equal to the prior. \square

B.12 Proof of Theorem 10.1 (Occlusion-Induced Ambiguity)

Theorem 10.1. Let \mathcal{G}_{\text{occluded}} \subset \mathcal{G} be the set of geometries consistent with the unoccluded observation. If the occlusion mask has positive measure, then |\mathcal{E}(o_t)| > 1 generically.

Proof. The observation map h_m: \mathcal{G} \times \mathcal{D} \to \mathcal{O} is generally non-injective when restricted to occluded regions. For any occlusion mask K \subset \mathcal{O} with \mu(K) > 0, there exist distinct (g, d) \neq (g', d') such that h_m(g, d) = h_m(g', d') on the visible region \mathcal{O} \setminus K. The set of such pairs is generic in the space of geometries and deformations, because the dimension of the occluded region's contribution to the observation is strictly less than the dimension of the state. \square

