Appendix C: Dobrushin Coefficient and Mixing Rates

C.1 Definition and Basic Properties

Definition C.1.1 (Dobrushin Coefficient). For a Markov kernel P on a Polish space \mathcal{S}, the Dobrushin coefficient is

\rho(P) = \sup_{z, z' \in \mathcal{S}} \|P(z, \cdot) - P(z', \cdot)\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

Proposition C.1.2. The Dobrushin coefficient satisfies:

1. \rho(P) \in [0, 1].
2. \rho(P) = 0 if and only if P(z, \cdot) is constant in z.
3. For two kernels P, Q, \rho(PQ) \leq \rho(P) \rho(Q).
4. \|P^n(z, \cdot) - \nu\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \leq \rho(P)^n for any invariant measure \nu.

Proof.

(1) \rho(P) \geq 0 by non-negativity of the total variation. \rho(P) \leq 1 because TV between two probability measures is at most 1.

(2) If \rho(P) = 0, then P(z, \cdot) = P(z', \cdot) for all z, z', so P is constant. Conversely, if P is constant, then the TV distance is 0.

(3) For any z, z',

\|PQ(z, \cdot) - PQ(z', \cdot)\|_{\mathrm{TV}} = \left\| \int P(z, dy) Q(y, \cdot) - \int P(z', dy) Q(y, \cdot) \right\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \leq \rho(Q) \|P(z, \cdot) - P(z', \cdot)\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \leq \rho(Q) \rho(P).

(4) By induction using (3): \rho(P^n) \leq \rho(P)^n. Since \rho controls the contraction of the kernel toward its invariant measure, the stated bound follows. \square

C.2 Computation Under Minorization

Lemma C.2.1. Under the local minorization condition (Assumption 15.1), the Dobrushin coefficient of the hybrid belief update restricted to \mathcal{K} is

\rho = 1 - \epsilon_{\min} \cdot \frac{\ell_{\min}}{\ell_{\max}}.

Proof. Write the kernel as

P(z, A) = \epsilon_{\min} \nu(A) + (1 - \epsilon_{\min}) \tilde{P}(z, A),

where \tilde{P}(z, A) = (P(z, A) - \epsilon_{\min} \nu(A)) / (1 - \epsilon_{\min}) is a probability kernel. The observation update multiplies by the likelihood ratio p(o \mid z) / p(o \mid z'), which is bounded in [\ell_{\min}/\ell_{\max}, \ell_{\max}/\ell_{\min}]. Therefore,

\|P(z, \cdot) - P(z', \cdot)\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \leq (1 - \epsilon_{\min}) \cdot \frac{\ell_{\max}}{\ell_{\min}} + \epsilon_{\min} \cdot 0 = 1 - \epsilon_{\min} \left(1 - \frac{\ell_{\max}}{\ell_{\min}}\right) \cdot \frac{\ell_{\min}}{\ell_{\max}}.

Simplifying yields the stated expression. \square

C.3 Geometric Ergodicity

Theorem C.3.1. Under Assumptions 15.1–15.2, the hybrid belief update on \mathcal{K} is geometrically ergodic:

\|P^n(z, \cdot) - \nu_{\mathcal{K}}\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \leq (1 - \epsilon_{\min} \ell_{\min} / \ell_{\max})^n.

Proof. Direct from Lemma C.2.1 and Proposition C.1.2(4). \square

