Appendix D: Conformal Prediction Details

D.1 Exchangeability and Rank Statistics

Definition D.1.1 (Exchangeability). A sequence V_1, \ldots, V_{n+1} is exchangeable if for every permutation \sigma of \{1, \ldots, n+1\},

(V_1, \ldots, V_{n+1}) \stackrel{d}{=} (V_{\sigma(1)}, \ldots, V_{\sigma(n+1)}).

Lemma D.1.2 (Rank Uniformity). If V_1, \ldots, V_{n+1} are exchangeable and almost surely distinct, then \mathrm{rank}(V_{n+1}) \sim \mathrm{Unif}\{1, \ldots, n+1\}.

Proof. By exchangeability, the distribution of (V_1, \ldots, V_{n+1}) is invariant under permutations. The rank of V_{n+1} is therefore uniformly distributed over \{1, \ldots, n+1\}. \square

D.2 Split Conformal Quantile

Definition D.2.1 (Split Conformal Quantile). Given calibration scores V_1, \ldots, V_n, the split conformal quantile at level 1-\alpha is

\hat{q}_{1-\alpha} = V_{(\lceil (1-\alpha)(n+1) \rceil)},

where V_{(k)} is the k-th smallest order statistic.

Proposition D.2.2. If the calibration and test scores are exchangeable, then

P(V_{n+1} \leq \hat{q}_{1-\alpha}) \geq 1 - \alpha.

Proof. By Lemma D.1.2, \mathrm{rank}(V_{n+1}) \sim \mathrm{Unif}\{1, \ldots, n+1\}. Therefore,

P(V_{n+1} \leq V_{(k)}) = P(\mathrm{rank}(V_{n+1}) \leq k) = \frac{k}{n+1}.

Setting k = \lceil (1-\alpha)(n+1) \rceil gives the result. \square

D.3 Stratified Calibration

Definition D.3.1 (Stratified Quantile). For each stratum s \in \mathcal{S}, let n_s = |\{i : s_i = s\}| and let V_1^{(s)}, \ldots, V_{n_s}^{(s)} be the calibration scores in stratum s. The stratified conformal quantile is

\hat{q}_s = V_{(\lceil (1-\alpha)(n_s + 1) \rceil)}^{(s)}.

Theorem D.3.2 (Stratified Coverage). If calibration and test scores within each stratum are exchangeable, then

P(z^* \in \mathcal{C}^{(s)}_{1-\alpha} \mid s^* = s) \geq 1 - \alpha.

Proof. Apply Proposition D.2.2 within each stratum. \square

D.4 Union Bound Across Strata

Corollary D.4.1. With probability at least 1 - \delta over the calibration draw,

P\!\left( \forall s \in \mathcal{S} : z^* \in \mathcal{C}^{(s)}_{1-\alpha} \mid s^* = s \right) \geq 1 - |\mathcal{S}| \alpha - |\mathcal{S}| \sqrt{\frac{\log(2|\mathcal{S}|/\delta)}{2 \min_s n_s}}.

Proof. Apply the DKW bound (Lemma 16.3) within each stratum and take a union bound over |\mathcal{S}| strata. \square

Remark D.4.2. The analytical consequence of Corollary D.4.1 is that achieving a finite-sample gap of \epsilon_s at confidence \delta requires n_s \geq \log(2|\mathcal{S}|/\delta) / (2 \epsilon_s^2) calibration points per stratum. For |\mathcal{S}| = 5, \epsilon_s = 0.02, \delta = 0.05, this gives n_s \geq \log(1000) / (2 \cdot 0.0004) \approx 8{,}628. This is an analytical requirement, not a measured quantity; whether such calibration data can be collected is an open empirical question.

