Appendix E: Decision-Theoretic Derivations

E.1 Belief-Space Value Function

Definition E.1.1 (Belief MDP). The belief MDP induced by the hybrid POMDP (\mathcal{Z}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{O}, T, R, \gamma) is (\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Z}), \mathcal{A}, Q, R_{\text{belief}}, \gamma), where:

· Q(\pi, a, d\pi') = p(\pi' \mid \pi, a) is the belief transition kernel.
· R_{\text{belief}}(\pi, a) = \mathbb{E}_{z \sim \pi}[R(z, a)].

Proposition E.1.2 (Bellman Optimality). The optimal value function V^* satisfies

V^*(\pi) = \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \left[ R_{\text{belief}}(\pi, a) + \gamma \int V^*(\pi') Q(\pi, a, d\pi') \right].

Proof. Standard dynamic programming argument on the belief MDP. The measurability of the integral follows from the measurability of the belief transition kernel. \square

E.2 Value of Information

Definition E.2.1 (Value of Perfect Information). For a belief \pi and an action a, the value of perfect information is

\text{VoPI}(\pi, a) = \mathbb{E}_{z \sim \pi}\left[ V^*(z) \right] - V^*(\pi),

where V^*(z) is the optimal value under full state knowledge.

Proposition E.2.2. \text{VoPI}(\pi, a) \geq 0 with equality if and only if \pi is a point mass.

Proof. By Jensen's inequality and convexity of the value function in the belief (standard result for POMDPs). Equality holds iff the belief is degenerate. \square

E.3 Value of Branch Preservation

Definition E.3.1 (Value of Branch Preservation). For a belief \pi with mode posterior w(m) and a collapsed belief \pi_{\text{collapsed}} = \delta_{m^*},

\text{VoBP}(\pi) = V^*(\pi) - V^*(\pi_{\text{collapsed}}).

Lemma E.3.2. If \pi is multimodal with modes m_1, m_2, then

\text{VoBP}(\pi) \geq \gamma \cdot \mathbb{E}_o\left[ \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \left( V^*(\tau(\pi, a, o)) - V^*(\pi_{\text{collapsed}}) \right) \right].

Proof. Direct from Theorem 19.1 and the definition of VoBP. \square

E.4 Abstention Rule Derivation

Theorem E.4.1 (Optimal Abstention). Under the regularity conditions of Lemma 5.2, abstention is optimal if and only if

|\mathcal{C}_{1-\alpha}| > \frac{V_{\text{abstain}}(\pi_t)}{\kappa \alpha}.

Proof. The proof proceeds by comparing the value of acting and abstaining.

Acting: The expected value of acting under belief \pi_t with prediction set \mathcal{C} is

V_{\text{act}} = R_{\text{belief}}(\pi_t, a_{\text{act}}) + \gamma \mathbb{E}_o[V^*(\tau(\pi_t, a_{\text{act}}, o))] - \kappa \alpha |\mathcal{C}|.

The last term is the expected penalty for miscoverage, proportional to the set size.

Abstaining: The value of abstaining is

V_{\text{abstain}} = \max_{a_{\text{info}}} \mathbb{E}_o[V^*(\tau(\pi_t, a_{\text{info}}, o))] - V^*(\pi_t).

Abstention is optimal when V_{\text{abstain}} > V_{\text{act}}, which simplifies to the stated condition. \square

