Appendix F: Identifiability Analysis Details

F.1 Fisher Information Matrix

Definition F.1.1. The Fisher information matrix for parameter \theta at observation o is

\mathbf{F}_\theta = \mathbb{E}_{o \sim p(\cdot \mid \theta)}\left[ \nabla_\theta \log p(o \mid \theta) \, \nabla_\theta \log p(o \mid \theta)^\top \right].

Proposition F.1.2 (Cramér–Rao Bound). For any unbiased estimator \hat{\theta},

\mathrm{Cov}(\hat{\theta}) \succeq \mathbf{F}_\theta^{-1}.

Proof. Standard result from estimation theory, contingent on differentiability of the log-likelihood and interchange of differentiation and integration. \square

F.2 Identifiability Conditions

Theorem F.2.1 (Local Identifiability). \theta is locally identifiable at \theta_0 if and only if \mathbf{F}_{\theta_0} is positive definite.

Proof. If \mathbf{F}_{\theta_0} is positive definite, then the map \theta \mapsto p(\cdot \mid \theta) is locally injective by the inverse function theorem: for any direction v \neq 0, the Fisher quadratic form v^\top \mathbf{F}_{\theta_0} v > 0 implies that the log-likelihood changes in direction v at second order, so \theta_0 and \theta_0 + \epsilon v produce distinct distributions for small \epsilon. Conversely, if \mathbf{F}_{\theta_0} is singular, there exists v \neq 0 with v^\top \mathbf{F}_{\theta_0} v = 0, implying \nabla_\theta \log p(o \mid \theta_0)^\top v = 0 almost surely, so \theta and \theta + \epsilon v produce the same distribution to first order. \square

F.3 Regularized Rank Test

Lemma F.3.1. Let \hat{\mathbf{F}}_\theta be the empirical Fisher matrix with \|\hat{\mathbf{F}}_\theta - \mathbf{F}_\theta\|_2 \leq \eta. Define

\hat{\mathbf{F}}_\theta^{(\lambda)} = \hat{\mathbf{F}}_\theta + \lambda \mathbf{I}, \quad r_{\text{eff}} = \#\{i : \sigma_i(\hat{\mathbf{F}}_\theta^{(\lambda)}) \geq \tau \sigma_{\max}(\hat{\mathbf{F}}_\theta^{(\lambda)})\}.

Then

|r_{\text{eff}}(\hat{\mathbf{F}}_\theta) - r_{\text{eff}}(\mathbf{F}_\theta)| \leq \#\{i : \sigma_i(\mathbf{F}_\theta) \in [\tau \sigma_{\max} - (\eta + \lambda), \tau \sigma_{\max} + (\eta + \lambda)]\}.

Proof. By Weyl's inequality, |\sigma_i(\hat{\mathbf{F}}_\theta^{(\lambda)}) - \sigma_i(\mathbf{F}_\theta)| \leq \eta + \lambda for all i. A singular value crossing the threshold \tau \sigma_{\max} changes the effective rank by at most one. The total change is bounded by the number of singular values in the interval. \square

Remark F.3.2. The bound is integer-valued, so it is informative only when the spectrum is well-separated near the threshold. If many singular values cluster near \tau \sigma_{\max}, the bound may exceed 1 and the rank test is unreliable — exactly the regime in which the identifiability verdict should be marked as "unknown."

