Appendix G: Numerical-Analysis Considerations (Analytical, Not Empirical)

The following are analytical statements about the numerical behavior of the algorithms, derived from the mathematics above. No numerical experiments have been performed to verify them; they are predictions of the theory, subject to empirical test.

G.1 Effective Sample Size

Definition G.1.1. The effective sample size (ESS) is

\text{ESS} = \frac{1}{\sum_{i=1}^N (w_t^{(i)})^2}.

Proposition G.1.2. \text{ESS} \in [1, N], with \text{ESS} = N when all weights are equal and \text{ESS} = 1 when one weight is 1.

Proof. By Cauchy–Schwarz, \sum_i (w_t^{(i)})^2 \geq 1/N when \sum_i w_t^{(i)} = 1, so \text{ESS} \leq N. The lower bound \text{ESS} \geq 1 follows from \sum_i (w_t^{(i)})^2 \leq \sum_i w_t^{(i)} = 1. \square

G.2 Computational Complexity (Analytical)

The per-step computational complexity of the fast-path algorithm, counted in floating-point operations, is bounded by:

T_{\text{step}} = O(N \cdot d) + O(K \cdot M) + O(M),

where N is the number of particles, d the state dimension, K the number of deformation rollouts, and M the number of contact modes. This is an analytical bound; the actual constant depends on the encoder architecture and hardware, which have not been specified.

G.3 Regularization Choice (Analytical Justification)

Proposition G.3.1. Under the assumptions of Lemma F.3.1, the choice \lambda = \sigma_{\max} \cdot \kappa_\lambda with \kappa_\lambda > 0 small ensures that the perturbation \eta + \lambda is dominated by the spectral gap of \mathbf{F}_\theta, provided the gap exceeds \sigma_{\max} \cdot \kappa_\lambda.

Proof. Direct from the bound in Lemma F.3.1: the interval width 2(\eta + \lambda) is small relative to the gap when \lambda is small. \square

This is a statement about the mathematics of the rank test; it does not assert that any particular choice of \lambda works on real data.

