I. Core Prior Art

Deformable Object State Estimation

Wi, Y., Florence, P., Zeng, A., & Fazeli, N. (2022). VIRDO: Visio-tactile Implicit Representations of Deformable Objects. arXiv preprint arXiv:2202.00868. https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.00868 

The foundational work introducing visuo-tactile implicit representations of deformable objects. VIRDO operates directly on visual point clouds and tactile reaction forces to predict object deformations subject to external contacts, with generalization to unseen contact formations and state estimation under partial visuo-tactile feedback.

Wi, Y., Florence, P., Zeng, A., & Fazeli, N. (2023). VIRDO++: Real-World, Visuo-tactile Dynamics and Perception of Deformable Objects. Conference on Robot Learning (CoRL), PMLR. 

The central baseline for the present work. VIRDO++ decouples the 3D geometric representation of deformable objects into an undeformed (nominal) shape signed-distance field (SDF) and a set of deformation fields, maintaining a belief distribution over object deformation via a particle filter-based inference algorithm . The state-estimation algorithm eliminates the need for privileged contact information, enabling practical real-world application. The AHLF hybrid state decomposition (g_t, d_t, \theta_t) directly refines VIRDO++'s latent representation, adding explicit material parameters \theta_t and discrete contact modes \mathcal{M}.

Support-Safe Hybrid Filtering

Papamichalis, M., & Ruane, R. (2026). Support-Safe Variational Hybrid Filtering for Contact-Mode and Sparse-Law Recovery. arXiv preprint arXiv:2605.16398. https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.16398 

Introduces VHyDRO, a variational hybrid dynamics learner that prevents branch loss in contact-rich robot dynamics by mixing the learned proposal with a feasible transition law before sampling and importance weighting, ensuring that every transition retained by the model-feasible carrier remains covered . VHyDRO jointly infers a continuous latent state and a discrete contact mode, and fits a sparse port-Hamiltonian law to each recovered regime . This is the direct methodological ancestor of AHLF's support-safe proposal, though VHyDRO addresses discrete contact-mode recovery for hybrid systems rather than continuous deformation ambiguity under occlusion.

Conformal Prediction in Contact-Rich Robotics

Anonymous (2026). Particle-Based Conformal Prediction for Contact-Aware Uncertainty Calibration in Stratified Configuration Spaces. Proceedings of Machine Learning Research (PMLR). 

Introduces CaPTURe (Calibrated Particle-sets for Trans-dimensional Uncertainty Representation), a geometry-aware, conformal prediction-based algorithm that generates probabilistically valid prediction regions of the unknown future system configuration using particle-based models of arbitrary fidelity . The method handles trans-dimensional uncertainty by incorporating stratified configuration spaces (free space, contact manifolds), and uses a calibration dataset of system transitions to locally calibrate motion uncertainty estimates . This is the closest prior work to AHLF's stratified conformal calibration layer, though CaPTURe's primary contribution is prediction-region generation for planning, not hybrid posterior calibration.

Fang, J., et al. (2026). Robustness without Wrinkles: Parallel Simulation and Robust MPC for Certified Deformable Manipulation. arXiv preprint arXiv:2506.14188. 

Introduces CORD-SLS, a real-time control method for safe deformable object manipulation, with a focus on ropes and cloth. The framework integrates a GPU-parallel differentiable simulator with contact smoothing, and uses conformal prediction to calibrate visual-feedback and perception-error bounds for MPC, producing reachable tubes that enable high-probability safe control . CORD-SLS establishes that conformal prediction can provide distribution-free coverage guarantees in deformable manipulation, but its primary contribution is safe control, not hybrid posterior calibration.

Visuotactile Stiffness Estimation

Yao, S., Han, J., Liu, B., Li, Y., Zhang, K., Franceschini, N., Marques, J. C., & Hauser, K. (2025). Estimating High-Resolution Neural Stiffness Fields Using Visuotactile Sensors. IEEE International Conference on Robotics and Automation (ICRA). DOI: 10.1109/ICRA55743.2025.11128319 

Develops an observation model for dense contact forces to estimate object stiffness using a visuotactile sensor and a dense force estimator, and proposes a neural Volumetric Stiffness Field (VSF) formulation that represents stiffness as a continuous function . The work establishes that high-resolution visuotactile sensors provide detailed contact information promising for inferring the physical properties of objects in contact . This directly informs AHLF's slow-path material parameter estimation, though Neural VSF does not maintain a belief over contact modes or deformation hypotheses.

