II. Foundational Theory: Measure-Theoretic Probability and Hybrid Systems

Del Moral, P. (2004). Feynman-Kac Formulae: Genealogical and Interacting Particle Systems with Applications. Springer, Probability and Its Applications.

The standard reference for branching and interacting particle system approximations of Feynman-Kac formulae, providing the convergence theory on which AHLF's Theorem 15.3 (filter convergence) is built. Del Moral establishes uniform convergence results under mixing conditions on the transition kernel , and the convergence rate O(\sqrt{\log N / N}) for particle approximations is derived within this framework.

Del Moral, P., & Miclo, L. (2000). Branching and interacting particle systems approximations of Feynman-Kac formulae with applications to non-linear filtering. Séminaire de Probabilités XXXIV, Lecture Notes in Mathematics, 1729, 1–145. Springer. 

The foundational paper establishing the convergence of branching particle systems to the nonlinear filtering equation. Provides the theoretical basis for the support-safe branching particle filter and the minorization-based convergence analysis.

Bain, A., & Crisan, D. (2009). Fundamentals of Stochastic Filtering. Springer, Stochastic Modelling and Applied Probability.

Standard reference for continuous-discrete filtering, measure-valued processes, and the Zakai and Kushner-Stratonovich equations. Provides the measure-theoretic foundations for the hybrid belief process \pi_t and the jump-diffusion dynamics.

