III. Foundational Theory: Conformal Prediction

Vovk, V., Gammerman, A., & Shafer, G. (2005). Algorithmic Learning in a Random World. Springer.

The foundational monograph on conformal prediction, establishing the exchangeability condition and the split-conformal calibration procedure that underlies AHLF's stratified coverage guarantees.

Angelopoulos, A. N., & Bates, S. (2023). A Gentle Introduction to Conformal Prediction and Distribution-Free Uncertainty Quantification. arXiv preprint arXiv:2107.07511. 

A self-contained tutorial providing a working understanding of conformal prediction and related distribution-free uncertainty quantification techniques . The derivation of the split-conformal quantile and the DKW-based finite-sample coverage gap (Lemma 16.3) follow the standard treatment in this reference.

Kaiser, T., & Herzog, P. (2025). A Tutorial on Distribution-Free Uncertainty Quantification Using Conformal Prediction. Advances in Methods and Practices in Psychological Science. 

Provides the rationale for prediction intervals with guaranteed marginal coverage, illustrating the construction through increasingly complex examples . The treatment of calibration scores and exchangeability within strata informs AHLF's stratified conformal procedure.

Lindemann, L., Zhao, Y., & Deshmukh, J. V. (2024/2025). Formal Verification and Control with Conformal Prediction. arXiv preprint arXiv:2409.00536. 

A survey designing formal verification and control algorithms for autonomous systems with safety guarantees using conformal prediction . Establishes the theoretical basis for using conformal prediction in control-theoretic settings, relevant to AHLF's decision-theoretic abstention rule.

---

IV. Foundational Theory: Markov Chain Mixing and Ergodicity

Dobrushin, R. L. (1956). Central limit theorem for nonstationary Markov chains. Theory of Probability & Its Applications, 1(1), 65–80.

The original derivation of the Dobrushin contraction coefficient for Markov kernels. The coefficient \rho(P) = \sup_{z, z'} \|P(z, \cdot) - P(z', \cdot)\|_{\mathrm{TV}} and its properties (Proposition C.1.2) follow this work.

Meyn, S. P., & Tweedie, R. L. (2009). Markov Chains and Stochastic Stability (2nd ed.). Cambridge University Press.

The standard reference for minorization conditions, drift conditions, and geometric ergodicity of Markov chains. The local minorization condition (Assumption 15.1) and drift condition (Assumption 15.2) are standard formulations from this text. The Doeblin minorization condition and the resulting geometric ergodicity bound (Theorem C.3.1) are derived within this framework .

---

V. Foundational Theory: Identifiability and Fisher Information

Rothenberg, T. J. (1971). Identification in parametric models. Econometrica, 39(3), 577–591.

The foundational treatment of local identifiability via the Fisher information matrix. The condition that \theta is locally identifiable if and only if \mathbf{F}_\theta is positive definite (Theorem F.2.1) follows this work.

Cobelli, C., & DiStefano, J. J. (1980). Parameter and structural identifiability concepts and ambiguities: a critical review and analysis. American Journal of Physiology, 239(1), R7–R24.

A comprehensive treatment of identifiability in physiological systems, including the distinction between structural and practical identifiability and the role of excitation. The contact-excitation requirement (Theorem 8.1) is a specialization of the general excitation condition for parameter identifiability.

Eisenberg, M. C., & Hayashi, M. A. L. (2014). Determining identifiable parameter combinations in nonlinear dynamic systems. Bulletin of Mathematical Biology, 76(10), 2443–2476.

Establishes that identifiability is a necessary condition for successful parameter estimation, and develops methods for determining identifiable parameter combinations . Provides the framework for the regularized Fisher rank test (Lemma 9.1).

